Here are a few ways to style header fonts in CSS, ranging from basic styling to using custom fonts from Google Fonts. ### 1. Basic Header Styling
This targets all standard HTML headers (`<h1>` through `<h6>`) and applies a clean, modern look. ```css
/* Target all header tags */
h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 {
  font-family: 'Helvetica Neue', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
  font-weight: 700; /* Bold */
  color: #333333;   /* Dark gray */
  line-height: 1. 2; /* Tighter spacing for headers */
  margin-bottom: 1rem;
}

/* Optional: Specific styling for the main title (h1) */
h1 {
  font-size: 2. 5rem;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 1px;
  color: #2c3e50;
}
```

### 2. Using Custom Fonts (Google Fonts)
To make your headers stand out, you can import a specific font family. Here is an example using **Poppins** and **Playfair Display**. **Step 1:** Add this to your HTML `<head>` section:
```html
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts. googleapis. com">
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts. gstatic. com" crossorigin>
<link href="https://fonts. googleapis. com/css2? family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Poppins:wght@600&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
```

**Step 2:** Add this to your CSS:
```css
/* Modern Sans-Serif for H2-H6 */
h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 {
  font-family: 'Poppins', sans-serif;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: #444;
}

/* Elegant Serif for the Main Title (H1) */
h1 {
  font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif;
  font-weight: 700;
  font-size: 3rem;
  color: #1a1a1a;
  text-align: center;
}
```

### 3. Styling a Specific Header Class
If you want to style only specific headers without affecting all of them, use a class. **HTML:**
```html
<h1 class="hero-title">Welcome to My Site</h1>
<h2>Regular Header</h2>
```

